## Why

Lucca asked (2026-11-01) to check Owen Gretzinger's "How I made our CI 20x faster (steal this)" (https://x.com/owengretzinger/status/2116291924219060319, article https://x.com/i/article/2116240180571172864, 2026-10-30) for Eon. Boardy cut PR CI from about 40 to 2 minutes, mostly through an internal coding agent:
- faster runners (Blacksmith);
- faster tools (Oxlint, a faster typechecker);
- tests sharded onto bigger machines, with more cores rather than more shards;
- no per-file app reload in tests (Vitest `isolate: false`);
- a dependency cache that is actually seeded from main;
- parallel jobs;
- affected-only tests with a nightly full run;
- format, lint and typecheck in a pre-push hook.

The CI bill rose from $230 to $4,300 a month.

Eon's proof is mostly local Nix, not hosted CI (AP-CI-001). It is still slow, and its slowness costs agent time. On 2026-11-01, eon-7cf0y.8's final proof (full `eon-test`, then `nix flake check` of every `orbit-*` real-PTY check, then `.#default`) ran for well over half an hour on a shared machine, and two Stop-reply checks timed out under load (eon-etgwm).

## Change (investigation first, AP-PERF-001)

1. Measure a baseline on one fixed candidate and machine state:
   - wall time and the top contributors for full `eon-test`;
   - `nix flake check` with its per-check build times;
   - `.#default`;
   - each review loop's checks.
2. For each lesson, say whether it applies to Eon and what it would save. Candidates:
   - **Affected-only proof.** Map the current Verification table to measured costs. It already gives planning/docs a narrow route and requires whole-workspace eon-test before code review, with the overseer owning the package build. Compare alternatives as proposals only; no reduced proof route is authorized by this investigation.
   - **Fewer rebuilds.** `orbit-*` checks share one build where possible, and a real-PTY run uses a release Orbit. See the real-PTY sensitivity memory.
   - **Cache seeding.** Shared `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` and Nix store reuse across agent worktrees; each worktree currently builds a fresh 6-8 GB target.
   - **Cores against parallelism.** On a shared machine, `--max-jobs 2 --cores 6` against load.
   - **A local pre-push hook** for fmt, clippy and `git diff --check`.
   - **Hosted runners** for the macOS proof (mac-proof.yml), only under AP-CI-001's cost gate.
3. Report the measured wins and propose a ranked set of follow-up beads. Write no code before Lucca picks.

## Contract

None. Any change to the Verification table in docs/AGENT-RULES.md is a rule change, and Lucca approves it.

## Acceptance

A comment on this bead with:
- the baseline (workload, machine load, times);
- each lesson's applicability and its estimated or measured saving;
- the proposed follow-ups.

Every speed claim has a measurement behind it.

## Scope boundary

Investigation and measurement only. No CI spend, no rule change, no implementation.

## Measurement boundary
Use one exact revision/component set, report cache warmth, machine/load, concurrency and reused versus rebuilt derivations, and distinguish timings from estimates. Coordinate the heavy slot before measurements; reuse existing proof logs where their identities are sufficient. A stalled or failed check is a failure observation, not a timing win. Investigation only: no hosted dispatch/spend, hook, cache configuration or durable tooling change without the separate selected follow-up.

